# Redis ACL file for JA4proxy
# Phase 236: Analytics Trust Boundary
# Phase 813: this file is a TEMPLATE -- Redis ACL files do not support
# shell-style ${VAR} substitution, so it is never mounted directly. See
# deploy/docker/redis-entrypoint.sh, which renders it (substituting each
# ${..._REDIS_PASSWORD} below from the matching /run/secrets/* file) to
# /tmp/redis_acl.conf before redis-server reads it. Naming this file with
# the raw, un-rendered placeholders is deliberate: it makes the original
# bug (a static bind-mount of this exact file, which Redis would then read
# completely literally) structurally impossible to reintroduce by
# accident -- there is no path in either compose file for a literal
# "redis_acl.conf" to exist again.
#
# user proxy — used by the Go proxy itself (cmd/ja4pd).
#   JA4PROXY-2026-0052 / JA4PROXY-2026-0043: config/proxy.yml's
#   redis.acl_users block already named this user (proxy_user: "proxy")
#   and scripts/redis-acl-setup.sh already defined this exact grant for a
#   live-ACL-update deployment path — acl_users.enabled was simply never
#   flipped to true, so the proxy silently authenticated as the disabled
#   "default" user instead (Phase 813's root-cause finding). Mirrors
#   scripts/redis-acl-setup.sh's permission set exactly, including
#   ~analytics:* (the proxy's own fire-and-forget XADD analytics publish,
#   per CLAUDE.md's Redis Streams decision) and +eval/+evalsha (the
#   sliding-window rate limiter's Lua script, per
#   docs/reference/REDIS_SCHEMA.md's Sorted Set + Lua entry). +ping added
#   after direct testing showed +@read/+@write do NOT imply PING in
#   Redis's ACL category system (PING is categorized @fast @connection) --
#   without it, scripts/start-poc.sh's own Redis healthcheck
#   (`redis-cli --user proxy ... ping`) would authenticate successfully
#   and still report failure.
#
#   Phase 814k (JA4PROXY-2026-0099): the grant below was under-scoped and
#   every gap was a SILENT fail-open (rate limiting, audit trail, event
#   stream, pub/sub config reload all dead). Each pattern/command was
#   verified against the Go proxy's actual Redis usage, not assumed:
#     - +script|load — SlidingWindowCount EVALSHA (internal/redis/client.go
#       loadScripts, ScriptLoad; sliding_window.lua)
#     - ~proxy:* — heartbeat proxy:heartbeat:{hostname} (cmd/ja4pd/main.go)
#     - ~session:* ~concurrent:* ~lifespan:* ~behavioral:burst:* —
#       TCPAnalyzer + sync-skip list (internal/redis/client.go maybeSync)
#     - ~audit:* — pipeline audit:last_score:{ip} (internal/security/pipeline.go)
#     - ~offense:* — OffenseCounter offense:{ip} (offense_counter.go)
#     - ~ban_cidr:* — RDAP enrichment ban_cidr:{cidr} (rdap_enrichment.go)
#     - ~return_visitor:* — TCPAnalyzer return_visitor:{ip}
#     - ~fp:* — TapConsumer reads fp:os:ip / fp:ja4t:ip (written by ja4tap)
#     - ~geoip:* — geoip:blocked_cidrs (cmd/ja4pd/main.go)
#     - ~events:connection ~webhooks:dlq — XADD/XRead on the real connection
#       stream + webhook DLQ (internal/config/loader.go, webhook/delivery.go)
#     - ~ja4proxy:dc:*:sync:* — cluster sync agent stream (sync/agent.go)
#     - +exists +incr +scan — Client.Exists/Incr/Scan (internal/redis/client.go)
#     - +zrange — Client.ZRangeScores issues ZRANGE ... WITHSCORES (one
#       command); there is no separate zrangewithscores command in Redis
#     - +xread +xgroup +xreadgroup +xack — webhook XRead + sync agent
#       XGroupCreateMkStream/XReadGroup/XAck
#     - +subscribe &config:* &ja4:* &geoip:* — PubSubHandler hot-reload
#       channels (internal/redis/pubsub.go)
user proxy on >${REDIS_PASSWORD} ~ratelimit:* ~ban:* ~ban_cidr:* ~beacon:* ~dns:* ~abuseipdb:* ~rdap:* ~ja4:* ~config:* ~analytics:* ~proxy:* ~session:* ~lifespan:* ~concurrent:* ~behavioral:burst:* ~audit:* ~offense:* ~return_visitor:* ~fp:* ~geoip:* ~events:connection ~webhooks:dlq ~ja4proxy:dc:*:sync:* +get +set +del +expire +ttl +exists +incr +scan +zadd +zrange +zrangebyscore +zremrangebyscore +zcard +zincrby +sadd +srem +sismember +smembers +hset +hget +hincrby +hgetall +pfadd +pfcount +xadd +xread +xgroup +xreadgroup +xack +evalsha +eval +script|load +ping +subscribe &config:* &ja4:* &geoip:*

# user exporter — used by oliver006/redis_exporter ONLY.
#   Phase 813: previously authenticated with no username at all (defaults
#   to "default", which is disabled below) via REDIS_PASSWORD/
#   REDIS_PASSWORD_FILE — silently broken in the same way as the proxy.
#   Needs read-only visibility plus a couple of introspection commands to
#   produce its metrics (memory/keyspace/replication stats, client count),
#   nothing else. +ping: see the proxy user's comment above -- +@read does
#   not imply it, and redis_exporter's own health/connectivity probing
#   needs it.
user exporter on >${EXPORTER_REDIS_PASSWORD} ~* +@read +config|get +client|list +ping -@admin

# user management — used by the management console.
#   ~* = may access any key (it needs to read config:*, ja4:*, ban:*, etc.)
#   This is intentionally broad; the management console is trusted. +ping:
#   see the proxy user's comment above -- +@read/+@write do not imply it.
user management on >${MANAGEMENT_REDIS_PASSWORD} ~* +@read +@write +ping -@admin

# user analytics — used by the analytics container ONLY.
#   Phase 814k (JA4PROXY-2026-0098): the original grant `resetkeys
#   ~analytics:*` was too narrow — it let the container touch ONLY its own
#   `analytics:*` output keys, but the consumer reads the connection event
#   stream the Go proxy writes (`events:connection`) via XGROUP CREATE /
#   XREADGROUP / XACK, and the threat-intel feed runner writes `ti_feed:*`
#   (blocklist_uuids, ban_ips, active_stix_ids, poll_state, runtime_enabled,
#   leader_lock, manual_poll_triggers). Without those two patterns the
#   analytics node crash-looped with NoPermissionError at xgroup_create —
#   device-dead in production posture.
#   Least privilege is PRESERVED: analytics still cannot read config:dial,
#   ja4:blocklist, ban:*, etc. — the only additions are the stream it must
#   consume and the feed keys it must produce. The command grant is an
#   EXPLICIT whitelist, not +@read/+@write: those broad categories let the
#   analytics user run FLUSHALL/FLUSHDB/KEYS and other @keyspace/@write
#   commands, which is not required and violates least privilege (verified
#   live against redis:7.4.9-alpine while rolling out the fix). The commands
#   below cover every redis call the analytics node issues (XADD/XGROUP/
#   XACK/XREADGROUP/XREAD/XREVRANGE on the streams, plus SET/HSET/SADD/
#   ZADD/PFADD/... on its own analytics:* and ti_feed:* keys); MULTI/EXEC/
#   DISCARD cover the pipeline() usage in the threat-intel feed state.
user analytics on >${ANALYTICS_REDIS_PASSWORD} resetkeys ~analytics:* ~events:connection ~ti_feed:* +get +set +hset +hget +hgetall +hdel +del +expire +incr +sadd +srem +smembers +zadd +zcard +zremrangebyrank +xadd +xrevrange +xread +xgroup +xreadgroup +xack +pfadd +ping +multi +exec +discard -@admin

# user ja4tap — used by the standalone Go TAP/SPAN sensor (cmd/ja4-tap) ONLY.
#   phase-809 (O-006): documented in docs/runbooks/tap_mode.md but missing
#   from this canonical file — a sensor deployed straight from this file had
#   no permissions and every write failed.
#   ~fp:* +set +expire = base grant. The sensor only ever SETs fp:os:ip:{ip},
#   fp:ja4t:ip:{ip}, and fp:ban_intent:ip:{ip}, all with a TTL (EXPIRE is
#   implicit in SET ... EX, but some clients issue it separately) — no other
#   command is ever needed for the default (unarmed) advisory-only posture.
#   Widen to ~ban:* only when arming enforcement (--enforce); see D-001's
#   Enforcer.Consider, which also GETs ban:{ip} before writing it (to avoid
#   overwriting an operator ban), so +get is required alongside +set once armed:
#     ACL SETUSER ja4tap on >${JA4TAP_REDIS_PASSWORD} ~fp:* ~ban:* +set +expire +get
#   Deliberately NOT granted: +scan, +keys — the sensor never needs to
#   enumerate keys, and per P-002, ~fp:*/~ban:* read access is itself
#   PII-bearing (IPs are embedded in the key names).
user ja4tap on >${JA4TAP_REDIS_PASSWORD} ~fp:* +set +expire

# user default — disable the unauthenticated default user.
#   Any client that does not authenticate gets no permissions at all.
user default off nopass ~* -@all
